/**
 * Day 82 — Phase 5 country pack 3, Caribbean and Central America: Mexico refinement (ROADMAP item 12,
 * after Cuba, Haiti/DR, Jamaica and Guatemala).
 *
 * Mexico had a 7-item timeline (Day 1) and, before 1815, only schematic rings: an Olmec heartland
 * and 'mesoamerica' fringe, a Toltec 'central-mexico' block, a four-keyframe Aztec Empire whose 1519
 * ring swallowed the Tarascans, Tlaxcala and Tututepec, the Maya civilisation's 'yucatan' box, and
 * from 1519 the Spanish Empire's 'new-spain' / 'new-spain-north' rings and Colonial Americas'
 * 'new-spain-core', which covered the whole country from the conquest (Yucatán before Montejo,
 * Baja California a century before Loreto). No Classic Maya kingdom, Monte Albán, Teotihuacan, the
 * Purépecha or any Postclassic Maya state was drawn. This module draws Mexico from the Olmec to 1815:
 *
 *   Olmec (redrawn) · Monte Albán (Zapotec) · Teotihuacan · Kaan (Dzibanche / Calakmul) ·
 *   B’aakal (Palenque) · Cobá · Xochicalco · Cacaxtla · El Tajín · Uxmal (Puuc) · Chichén Itzá ·
 *   Tollan / Toltecs (redrawn) · Mixtec kingdoms (Mixteca) · Tututepec · Mayapán · Zaachila /
 *   Tehuantepec · Purépecha (Tarascan) state · Tlaxcala · Metztitlán · Aztec Triple Alliance
 *   (redrawn) · Maya provinces of Yucatán · New Spain (Mexico's part)
 *
 * From 1815 the nations layer takes over (New Spain, then Mexico), so no keyframe here is later
 * than 1815. Independence, the US war, the Caste War, the Reform, the Revolution and 1968–2000 are
 * timeline items with pins.
 *
 * How it is built (docs/DATA.md, "Country-pack globe backfill"):
 *  - Mexico is cut into 39 pieces (MX_PIECES, plus ALL, their union): Natural Earth 1:10m states
 *    (public domain), some grouped (Zacatecas + Aguascalientes, Jalisco + Colima, Guanajuato +
 *    Querétaro, México + the Federal District) and some cut on lines (Hidalgo at 20.35°N; Veracruz
 *    at 20°N and 18.6°N; Oaxaca into Mixteca, coast, centre and Isthmus; Chiapas into the north /
 *    Lacandon, the highlands and Soconusco; Campeche at 19°N; Yucatán at 89°W; Quintana Roo at
 *    19.4°N), clipped to the globe's own Mexico land (src/data/nations.topo.json shape 420),
 *    simplified together as one coverage so neighbouring pieces share edges, and cut back from
 *    Guatemala's Day 81 pieces so the two packs never overlap at the border. Generator: et-tools
 *    day82-pieces.py (outside the repo); the output is plain coordinates.
 *  - A polity is a dated list of pieces; every change is a one-year step. A polity can have more
 *    than one region (the Aztec core and tributary provinces are drawn apart, so Tlaxcala, which
 *    they ringed, is not a hole; Soconusco and Baja California are separate while cut off).
 *  - The Spanish Empire's 'new-spain' and 'new-spain-north' regions and Colonial Americas'
 *    'new-spain-core' are dropped (MX_DROPS): New Spain is drawn once, by this pack, piece by piece.
 *    The Spanish borderlands north of today's border (Texas, New Mexico, the Californias) wait for
 *    a United States refinement; until then they are left to the peoples layer before 1815.
 *  - The Maya civilisation ring and the lidar / peoples / presence layers are cultural, not states,
 *    and stay underneath.
 *  - Pieces are regions, not surveyed borders. Classic kingdoms are drawn at their core domain,
 *    not their shifting hegemonies (see each polity's confidence reason).
 */

import { ensureClockwise } from './globe-morph.js';
import { mergeRings } from './globe-med-pieces.js';

const A = 'simplified';
const W = (title) => ({ title: `Wikipedia — ${title}`, url: `https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/${encodeURI(title.replace(/ /g, '_')).replace(/'/g, '%27')}` });

